# Buttons

Use a button to change something or submit an answer, and name the outcome in its label: “Save schedule”, not “Submit”.

## When to use it

Use one main action for each local task, with quieter secondary actions. A household dashboard can hold several independent device tasks, so it does not need one primary button for the whole home. This page owns the button contract for every Civica example. The [forms overview](controls.html#choose-the-control-by-its-meaning) helps choose between a command, a selection and navigation.

There are four variants: primary, secondary, destructive and unavailable. They share the same request wording and visible focus.

## When not to use it

Use a link to go somewhere. Do not make every room tile look like a submit button. Do not use a red button only because the theme colour is Red. Destructive confirmation uses explicit wording and the fixed danger role, not the theme colour.

## Say what was sent, not what happened

After a command, say that a request was sent until the integration reports the result. “Turning on reading lamp” is a request. “Reading lamp is on” is a state. Keep the native Home Assistant service permissions and pending states. The examples below only change their own text.

## How it behaves

Use a real button element. Give a non-submit control `type="button"`. Keep a visible focus indicator and make repeated actions at least 44px high. Explain an unavailable action beside the control, not through a faded appearance. Keep focus in place while a request completes.

